-- title:
--   Order of the Eisenstein-primary q^m-torsion is a q-power
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ and $q$ be primes and let $m$ be a natural number. Consider $J_0(p)$ in the form `JZero p`, the degree-zero Picard group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the function field `modularFunctionFieldBar p` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, equipped with the module structure `heckeModuleBar p` over the Hecke algebra `HeckeAlg` $= \mathbb{Z}[X_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$ (the structure obtained from the evaluation `heckeEvalBar` of the polynomial variables at the Hecke operators when these commute, and from evaluation of all variables at $0$ otherwise). Inside it sits the subgroup `eisensteinPrimaryTorsionBar p q m`, defined as the intersection of the kernel of multiplication by the integer $q^m$ with the supremum, over all $k \in \mathbb{N}$, of the submodules annihilated by the $k$-th power of the Eisenstein maximal ideal `eisensteinMaximalIdeal p q`, the latter being the preimage under the ring homomorphism `eisensteinEval p` of the ideal of $\mathbb{Z}$ generated by $q$. The assertion is that there exists $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $\mathrm{Nat.card}$ of this subgroup equal to $q^n$; since `Nat.card` vanishes on infinite types, this includes the finiteness of the subgroup.
--
--   This is the elementary statement that the $\mathfrak{P}$-primary part of the $q^m$-torsion of the Jacobian of $X_0(p)$ is a finite abelian $q$-group, $\mathfrak{P}$ being the Eisenstein maximal ideal above $q$. It is used in the study of the primary torsion of the Néron model of $J_0(p)$, in particular by [`ModularCurve.JZeroNeronPrimaryTorsionCore.exists_natCard_fppfCohomology_zero_eq_pow`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JZeroNeronPrimaryTorsionCore.exists_natCard_fppfCohomology_zero_eq_pow) and [`ModularCurve.nonempty_jZeroNeronPrimaryTorsionInvPins`](thm.html#ModularCurve.nonempty_jZeroNeronPrimaryTorsionInvPins).
